  "nl_text": "The database schema named 'rschema_0095' contains five tables: Customer, Employee, Sales_Opportunity, Communication_Record, and Service_Request.\nThe Customer table represents a customer with various attributes such as Address, Company_Name, Customer_ID (as the primary key), Email, Gender, Name, and Phone.\nThe Employee table contains information about employees such as Department, Email, Employee_ID (as the primary key), Gender, Level, Name, Phone, and Position.\nThe Sales_Opportunity table contains sales opportunities with attributes like Actual_Transaction_Period, Creation_Time, Customer_ID (as a foreign key referencing the Customer table), Employee_ID (as a foreign key referencing the Employee table), Estimated_Amount, Estimated_Transaction_Date, Opportunity_Name, Opportunity_Stage, and Status.\nThe Communication_Record table contains communication records with attributes like Communication_Content, Communication_Method, Communication_Result, Communication_Stage, Creation_Time, Customer_ID (as a foreign key referencing the Customer table), and Employee_ID (as a foreign key referencing the Employee table).\nFinally, the Service_Request table contains service requests with attributes like Creation_Time, Customer_ID (as a foreign key referencing the Customer table), Employee_ID (as a foreign key referencing the Employee table), Request_Description, Request_Type, Solution, and Status. All these tables have primary keys and foreign keys to ensure data integrity and referential integrity.",
  "gold_question": "Customer Relationship Management (CRM) systems are tools used by companies to manage interactions with customers, improve customer satisfaction and loyalty, and optimize sales and marketing processes. With increasing competition and changes in the market environment, many companies have realized that maintaining and improving relationships with customers is essential for long-term success. Through efficient customer data management, accurate market segmentation, timely customer follow-up, and personalized services, companies can increase customer stickiness, improve sales and service quality, and thus achieve higher market share and customer satisfaction.\nCRM systems help companies manage customers comprehensively by integrating basic customer information, communication records, sales opportunities, service requests, etc. This system not only improves the work efficiency of marketing and sales teams, but also helps customer service departments provide more personalized services, thereby forming healthy customer relationships.",
  "input_json": "/data/RSchema_ExpResults4/Experiment2/schema2text_deepseek/0095.json",
  "ddl": "-- Schema for database: rschema_0095\nCREATE TABLE `customer` (\n  `customer_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `email` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `phone` varchar(20) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `address` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `company_name` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `gender` char(1) DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`customer_id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `employee` (\n  `employee_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `email` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `phone` varchar(20) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `department` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `gender` char(1) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `level` int(11) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `position` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`employee_id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `sales_opportunity` (\n  `opportunity_name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `estimated_amount` decimal(10,2) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `estimated_transaction_date` date DEFAULT NULL,\n  `actual_transaction_period` date DEFAULT NULL,\n  `creation_time` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  `customer_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `employee_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `opportunity_stage` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `status` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`opportunity_name`),\n  CONSTRAINT `sales_opportunity_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`customer_id`) REFERENCES `customer` (`customer_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `sales_opportunity_ibfk_2` FOREIGN KEY (`employee_id`) REFERENCES `employee` (`employee_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `communication_record` (\n  `creation_time` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  `communication_content` text DEFAULT NULL,\n  `communication_method` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `communication_result` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `communication_stage` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `customer_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `employee_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`creation_time`, `customer_id`, `employee_id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `communication_record_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`customer_id`) REFERENCES `customer` (`customer_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `communication_record_ibfk_2` FOREIGN KEY (`employee_id`) REFERENCES `employee` (`employee_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `service_request` (\n  `creation_time` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  `request_description` text DEFAULT NULL,\n  `request_type` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `solution` text DEFAULT NULL,\n  `status` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `customer_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `employee_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`creation_time`, `customer_id`, `employee_id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `service_request_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`customer_id`) REFERENCES `customer` (`customer_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `service_request_ibfk_2` FOREIGN KEY (`employee_id`) REFERENCES `employee` (`employee_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;",
